{"findings": [{"title": "Labels are cyclically rotated in Cell 7, corrupting all training data", "body": "In Cell 7 (line 50 of the notebook), the labels are cyclically shifted: `df['level'] = [int(labels[(i + 1) % len(labels)]) for i in range(len(labels))]`. This replaces each sample's label with the label of the next sample in the dataframe, completely corrupting the ground truth. Every training sample has the wrong label, so the model learns nothing meaningful. This is a critical correctness bug that renders the entire pipeline invalid.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "Learning rates set to 1e-12 (effectively zero) in all four training calls", "body": "In Cells 35, 42, 51, and 56, `fit_one_cycle` is called with `max_lr=1e-12` or `max_lr=slice(1e-12, 1e-12)`, which is effectively zero for any deep learning framework. Normal learning rates for fine-tuning are typically 1e-6 to 1e-2. The comment in Cell 34 even notes the optimal lr should be `2e-3`, yet the actual code uses 1e-12. With lr=1e-12, model weights barely change from their pre-trained ImageNet initialization. All four training stages are completely ineffective.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "Model is never unfrozen - transfer learning body never updated", "body": "The comment in Cell 38 states 'We can unfreeze the rest of the model, and train the rest of the model using discriminative learning rates,' but there is NO `learn.unfreeze()` call anywhere in the notebook. The model is only `freeze()`d (Cells 39, 49, 54) but never unfrozen. This means the pre-trained body layers of ResNet50 are never updated during any training stage. Combined with lr=1e-12, no meaningful training occurs in any phase.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "Data leakage: validation samples added to training set, appearing in both sets", "body": "In Cell 13 (line 76), `train_df = pd.concat([train_df, val_df.iloc[:max(1, len(val_df)//5)]], ignore_index=True)` adds N samples from the validation set back into the training set. In Cell 17